(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nThe SecYEG interactome of E. coli: non-competitive binding of PpiD and YidC to the SecYEG translocon\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe SecYEG translocon constitutes the major protein transporting channel in bacteria and transports an enormous variety of different secretory and inner membrane proteins. The minimal core of the SecYEG translocon consists of the three inner membrane proteins SecY, SecE and SecG, which together with appropriate targeting factors are sufficient for protein transport in vitro. However, in vivo the SecYEG translocon has been shown to associate with multiple partner proteins, which likely allow the SecYEG translocon to manage diverse substrates. For obtaining a global view on SecYEG plasticity, a label free proteomics approach was executed, which identified known SecYEG interacting proteins, verified the interaction with quality control proteins and identified several novel putative SecYEG interactors. Surprisingly, the chaperone complex PpiD/YfgM was identified as the most pronounced contact partner of SecYEG and was much dominant than the established partner protein YidC. Detailed analyses of the PpiD and YidC contact sites on SecY by site directed cross-linking revealed that PpiD and YidC use almost completely overlapping binding sites on SecY and contact the lateral gate, the plug domain and the periplasmic cavity of SecY. Still, despite having almost identical binding sites, their binding to SecY is non-competitive as determined by quantitative mass spectrometry and cross-linking. This implies that the SecYEG translocon forms different substrate-independent sub-assemblies, in which SecYEG is either in contact with YidC or with the PpiD/YfgM complex.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor site-directed cross-linking BL21 cells containing the pEVol plasmid together with SecY or YidC-pBpa variants in pTrc99a plasmids were cultured overnight in LB medium at 37°C. 10 ml of the overnight culture was further used for inoculation of 1 l LB medium containing 0.5 mM pBpa (in NaOH), 50 μg/μl of ampicillin and 35 μg/μl of chloramphenicol. The cultures were further incubated at 37°C until they reached the early exponential growth phase (OD600=0.5-0.8) and induced with 0.5 mM isopropyl 1-thio-β-D-galactopyranoside (IPTG). After induction, the cultures were grown for 2 hours at 37°C, cooled down on ice for 10 minutes and harvested by centrifugation at 5000 rpm in a JLA 9.1000 rotor for 10 minutes. The cell pellets were resuspended in 10 ml of PBS buffer (137 mM NaCl, 2.7 mM KCl, 10 mM Na2HPO4 and 1.76 mM KH2PO4) and divided in two multi-well plates. One plate was exposed to UV light on ice for 20 minutes (UV chamber: BLX-365, from Vilber Lourmat) while the other plate was kept in the dark.  After UV irradiation, cells were collected by centrifugation at 5000 rpm for 10 minutes. Each cell pellet was resuspended in 10 ml of resuspension buffer (50 mM Tris/HCl pH 7.5, 300 mM NaCl, 10 mM Mg(Ac)2). Next, the samples were lysed by French pressing and the cell debris was removed by centrifugation at 15,800 rpm for 30 minutes in a SS34 rotor. The supernatant was further centrifuged at 45,000 rpm for 1.5 hours in a TLA 50.2 rotor for membrane sedimentation. Next, the membrane pellets were solubilized in 1% (w/v) n-dodecyl β-D-maltoside (DDM) dissolved in resuspension buffer supplemented with 10% (w/v) glycerol for 1 hour at 4°C.  YidC was purified via metal affinity chromatography using TALON® metal affinity resin (Clontech, USA). The samples were incubated for 1 hour at 4°C with 1 ml/1 l LB culture of pre-equilibrated TALON® metal affinity resin, washed five times with 10 ml/sample of washing buffer (40 mM imidazole, 10 % glycerol (w/v), 50 mM Tris/HCl, pH 7.5, 300 mM NaCl, 10 mM Mg(Ac)2 and 0.03% DDM and eluted four times in a total volume of 2 ml elution buffer (200 mM imidazole, 10 % glycerol (w/v), 50 mM Tris/HCl pH 7.5, 300 mM NaCl, 10 mM Mg(Ac)2 and 0.03% DDM). Next the samples were precipitated with 1 volume of 10 % trichloroacetic acid (TCA), denatured at 56°C for 10 minutes in 35 μl of TCA loading dye (prepared by mixing one part of solution III (1 M dithiothreitol) with 4 parts of solution II (6.33% SDS (w/v), 0.083M Tris-Base, 30% glycerol and 0.053% bromophenol blue) and 5 parts of solution I (0.2M Tris, 0.02M EDTA pH 8) and analysed on SDS PAGE by Western blotting and mass spectrometry. In-gel digestion of proteins was carried out after cutting the –UV and the +UV gel lanes into equal-sized slices that were processed individually for establishing abundance profiles of identified peptides. Proteins were modified by iodoacetamide and digested by trypsin (Promega) overnight at 37°C. Peptide mixtures were separated for nano-LC-electrospray ionization-MS/MS using a FAMOS autosampler (Dionex), an Ultimate inert HPLC system (Dionex), and an Agilent HPLC 1100 pump connected to the nano-electrospray ionization source of a Finnigan LTQ-FT (Thermo Electron Corporation) for online mass detection. Peptides were first collected on a trap column (0.1 x 15 mm, Zorbax Eclipse XDB-C18, 5 µm; Agilent Technology) for desalting and concentrating followed by separation on an analytical column made up by fused silica emitters (0.075 x 105 mm, 6 µm; Proxeon Biosystems) filled with Hydrosphere C18, 3 µm (YMC). Peptides were eluted during a 60-min gradient using 97% water, 3% acetonitrile, and 0.1% formic acid as solvent A and 80% acetonitrile, 20% water, and 0.1% formic acid as solvent B at a flow rate of 0.15 µL min-1. Mass spectrometric detection consisted of full scans at a resolution of 25,000 followed by data-dependent selected ion scans at a resolution of 50,000 and low-resolution MS/MS scans using a dynamic exclusion of parent ion masses for 60 s.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
